          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does COPD guidance describe about when to add an inhaled corticosteroid?",
          "answer": "The COPD guidance (*COPD: spirometric diagnosis, group assessment and initial therapy*, Global respiratory strategy report) describes adding an inhaled corticosteroid in a specific situation:\n\n- **Who it applies to:** people with a significant history of flare-ups (exacerbations). For them, the guidance first describes dual long-acting bronchodilator therapy. An inhaled corticosteroid is added where the blood eosinophil count is high, or where features of coexisting asthma are present.\n- **Why a blood marker is used:** the guidance says the blood eosinophil count predicts who benefits. It is not applied to everyone, because inhaled corticosteroids in this population increase the risk of pneumonia. The benefit has to be identifiable to be worth that risk.\n\nThe retrieved passage is an excerpt that begins partway through the section, so I cannot describe the full sequence of steps or any thresholds. The same document notes that stopping smoking is the only intervention shown to change the rate of lung function decline.\n\nWhether an inhaled corticosteroid suits a particular person depends on their own history and test results, so that is a question for their clinician. The asthma guidance also came up in the search, but it covers a different condition and I have not used it here.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
